 This family comprises methods to extract explicit triangular meshes from trained 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) models [G]. Standard 3DGS represents scenes as implicit volumetric primitives, lacking a native surface representation [G]. These methods solve the problem of obtaining watertight, textured geometry for downstream applications like simulation, AR/VR, or editing [G]. They differ from sibling methods like [Radiative and Tomographic Gaussians](4971-radiative-and-tomographic-gaussians.md) by focusing on geometric extraction rather than physical property rendering, and from [Domain-Specific Adaptations](5621-domain-specific-adaptations.md) by targeting general mesh generation rather than specific domain constraints like microscopy [G].
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family operate on the output of a trained 3DGS model $\mathcal{G} = \{G_i\}_{i=1}^M$ [G]. Each primitive $G_i$ is defined by position $\mathbf{p}_i$, covariance $\mathbf{\Sigma}_i$, opacity $\alpha_i$, and color attributes, as detailed in [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md). The core challenge is converting these anisotropic, volumetric kernels into a discrete surface representation [G]. This requires either evaluating the implicit density field defined by the Gaussians, leveraging geometric cues (depth/normal) rendered from the splats, or modifying the splat distribution to align with an existing mesh [G]. The rendering pipeline's geometric projection remains consistent with the parent family: 3D Gaussians are transformed into ray space, and the projected 2D covariance $\mathbf{\hat{\Sigma}}_i$ is computed using the Jacobian $J$ of the affine approximation of the projection and the rotation part $W$ of the extrinsic matrix [Radiative Gaussian Splatting](3040-radiative-gaussian-splatting.md).
