 ## Background
 The extraction of meshes from 3DGS is a necessary step for integrating neural radiance fields and Gaussian splats into traditional computer graphics pipelines [G]. Early 3DGS implementations focused solely on novel view synthesis [G]. Subsequent research identified the lack of explicit geometry as a limitation for applications requiring collision detection or texture mapping [G]. Methods like GOF [3692-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof-mesh-extraction.md] and Poisson-based extraction [12790-mesh-extraction-via-poisson-reconstruction.md] emerged to bridge this gap, while distance-based approaches [7411-distance-based-gaussian-splatting.md] addressed geometric consistency in large-scale scenes [G].
